  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nBackbuilding thunderstorm complex developed near the Indiana/Ohio \nState line - at the nose of a 30 KT westerly low level jet, where \nfavorable moisture advection exists. The airmass feeding these storms\nis very moist with PWAT values in excess of 1.8 inches. The overall system\nmovement has remained quasi-stationary with widespread daily rainfall\namounts of 2 to 5 inches across a good portion of eastern Indiana. \nSome locations across Fayette and Franklin counties have received \n6 to 8+ inches. \n\nThese elevated storms are moving south-southeast and the eastern\nextent is becoming more scattered in coverage but backbuilding\ncontinues to be evident. An upstream shortwave will result in \nadditional thunderstorm development this afternoon across the western\nperiphery of the initial complex. Potential for another 2 to 4 \ninches of rainfall with locally higher amounts. Have continued the \nflood watch for flash flooding. Outflow from the initial thunderstorm\ncomplex and associated cold pool will likely limit the eastern \nextent of the severe weather threat. Our severe weather threat today \nhas decreased but can not rule out isolated strong to damaging wind \ngusts across the far western counties. \n\nActivity is expected to diminish early this evening as the system \ndrops south of ILN/s FA. Some fog and stratus development is likely \novernight in the wake of the rain with low levels being very \nsaturated.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nWeak shortwaves embedded in the northwest flow aloft will offer\nadditional rainfall chances on Thursday, Friday and Saturday. The \nmain threat from these clusters of storms will be periods of heavy \nrain and potential flooding.\n\nHeading into the weekend, the ridge builds north, allowing temperatures\nto increase and precipitation chances to decrease on Sunday. Another\ntrough will allow a surface cold front to slip south through the \narea early Monday. Based CAPE and shear overlap there may be a \nrenewed chance for severe weather. Drier air is expected to filter \nin behind the front later Monday and Tuesday.",
